    q: "What is DFS, and what problem does it solve?",
    a: "Distributed File System has two parts: DFS Namespaces, presenting file shares from multiple servers under a single unified logical path so users don't need to know which physical server hosts what; and DFS Replication, keeping copies of that data in sync across servers/sites for redundancy and local access speed at branch offices. It decouples the logical path users see from the physical server layout, so data can move between servers without breaking mapped drives/shortcuts.",
