    a: "The shebang (#!/bin/bash as the very first line) tells the OS which interpreter to run the script with when executed directly. Every command returns an exit code — 0 means success, non-zero means some kind of failure. $? holds the last command's exit code; a script returns its own exit code via exit N (or the last command's code if omitted) — this is how calling scripts/cron/CI pipelines detect whether your script actually succeeded, not just whether it ran.",
