    lvl: "hard",
    q: "You're asked to reduce a Windows file server's attack surface. List concrete steps beyond just patching.",
    sol: "Disable SMBv1 entirely, since it's a legacy, vulnerable protocol with no reason to remain enabled on a modern network. Restrict NTLM in favor of Kerberos where possible. Remove unused server roles/features. Enforce least-privilege NTFS/share permissions rather than broad 'Everyone: Full Control' shares. Enable audit logging on sensitive shares. Restrict administrative access (RDP/management ports) to a jump host or specific management subnet rather than allowing it from the general LAN.",
    exp: "Reducing attack surface means removing/restricting things that don't need to be there — old protocols, unused roles, over-broad permissions, unrestricted admin access paths — not just staying current on patches for what remains enabled.",
