    cat: "Systems",
    lvl: "hard",
    q: "Design a disaster recovery plan for a company's primary AD domain controller failing catastrophically (hardware failure, unrecoverable), given they have a second DC in the same site.",
    sol: "With a second healthy DC, this isn't a domain-wide disaster since AD is multi-master for almost everything except FSMO roles. Steps: 1) Confirm or seize any FSMO roles that were on the failed DC on the surviving DC (seize rather than transfer, since the failed DC can't gracefully hand them off). 2) Cleanly remove the dead DC's metadata from AD so replication/DNS stop trying to reach a server that's gone. 3) Update DNS/DHCP scope options and anything hardcoded pointing at the dead DC's IP. 4) Build/promote a new DC to restore full redundancy as soon as possible. Never restore a dead DC from a backup/snapshot older than the tombstone lifetime (default 180 days) without care, since it risks reintroducing stale objects or USN rollback issues.",
    exp: "The key insight interviewers look for is recognizing that a second healthy DC means this isn't a true domain-wide disaster thanks to multi-master replication — the real work is FSMO role recovery and clean metadata removal, not a full domain rebuild.",
