    q: "Write a Bash script that checks if a website is reachable via HTTP and restarts a local nginx service if it returns anything other than a 200 status, with basic retry logic to avoid restarting on a single transient blip.",
    sol: '#!/bin/bash\nURL=\'http://localhost/health\'\nFAILS=0\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  CODE=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w \'%{http_code}\' "$URL")\n  if [ "$CODE" != \'200\' ]; then\n    FAILS=$((FAILS+1))\n  fi\n  sleep 5\ndone\nif [ "$FAILS" -ge 3 ]; then\n  echo "$(date): health check failed $FAILS/3 times, restarting nginx" >> /var/log/health-watchdog.log\n  systemctl restart nginx\nfi',
    exp: "Requiring 3 consecutive failures, not just one, before restarting avoids flapping/restarting a healthy service on a single transient network blip — a naive single-check-and-restart script causes more instability than it prevents.",
