    q: "Design a backup strategy (schedule, retention, storage) for a 500GB file server, given a business requirement of RPO under 24 hours and the ability to restore any file as it existed up to 90 days ago.",
    sol: "Schedule: a weekly full backup plus daily incremental backups the rest of the week, meeting the under-24h RPO since the worst case is losing one day's changes. Retention: keep the daily incrementals for the current week and weekly fulls for the last 90 days, enabling a restore to any point in that window, though restoring a mid-week file from several weeks back requires that week's full plus its incrementals up to that day. Storage: primary backup to on-prem/NAS storage for fast restores of recent data, with a copy replicated or synced off-site to satisfy the 3-2-1 rule.",
    exp: "A backup strategy is really an equation with the business's stated RPO and retention requirements on one side and schedule/retention/storage choices on the other — the design should be derived directly from those numbers, not from a generic best-practice template applied without checking it meets the stated requirements.",
