    q: "Your organization was hit by ransomware that encrypted the primary file server and its locally-attached backup drive. Design a backup architecture change to prevent this specific failure mode from recurring.",
    sol: "The root cause is that the backup was reachable/writable from the same compromised network path as the production data. Fix: add the offline/air-gapped leg of the 3-2-1-1-0 rule — at least one backup copy on tape rotated out of the drive, or immutable/object-lock cloud storage that ransomware executing on the network cannot reach or overwrite even with full credentials — so at least one copy survives regardless of what happens on the network. Also review the backup service account's permissions, since an account with domain-admin-equivalent rights across the whole environment is itself a risk if ransomware compromises that account specifically to reach the backups.",
    exp: "Ransomware increasingly targets backups deliberately, since encrypting the backup too removes the victim's ability to simply restore and ignore the ransom demand — an air-gapped or immutable copy specifically defeats that, which is why it's now considered a baseline requirement, not an optional extra.",
