    q: "Design a disaster recovery test plan (a tabletop or live DR test) for a company that has a documented DR plan but has never actually tested it, given they claim a 4-hour RTO for their core ERP system.",
    sol: "Start with a tabletop exercise, walking through the plan on paper with no systems touched, to find obvious gaps first — outdated contact info, a runbook referencing a decommissioned server, an assumption that a specific person who left the company would perform a step. Then run a live, scheduled test in a maintenance window: actually fail over to the DR environment, or restore to an isolated test environment if a live failover is too risky, and time the real process end-to-end against the claimed 4-hour RTO, since most orgs discover their real RTO is significantly longer than documented the first time they actually test it. Document every gap found, fix them, and schedule the next test, since DR plans decay as the environment changes.",
    exp: "An untested DR plan is really just a DR hypothesis — the whole point of this exercise is discovering that the real RTO doesn't match the documented one before a real disaster forces that discovery under pressure.",
